  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1243 PM EDT Sun Aug 9 2026\n\nFocus remains on convective evolution and intensity late this \nafternoon into tonight. Surface warm front is on schedule in \nreaching to near the MI border during this time, allowing for a \nmuggy and unstable air mass to settle in for most locations under \nperturbed west-northwest flow aloft. \n\nThere still appears to be two windows for convection, though \nthere might not be much of a break between them. The first \nwindow is late this afternoon into early this evening as a small\nscale bowing segment and MCV (currently near Milwaukee) \ncontinues east-southeast interacting with the warm front and \nsubstantial MUCAPE axis. New, scattered, sfc based cell growth \nappears likely along its outflow in the vicinity of the MI state\nline 20-01z. Marginal deep layer shear profiles expected here \ninitially, along with forcing and colder temps aloft bypassing \nnorth may allow weak capping to limit coverage and intensity \nsomewhat. That said, ~2000 j/kg of MLCAPE and the current \nforward momentum of the cluster could support a damaging wind \nand non-zero tornado threat into mainly southwest MI and far \nnorthern IN during this time.\n\nThe second window comes later this evening into the overnight hours \nas the warm front becomes invigorated by an incoming small scale \nimpulse and deeper moisture advection on the nose of a ~40 kt \nlow level jet. There still may be a damaging wind threat with \nany bowing segments given the lingering MUCAPE and increasing \ndeep layer shear, with an isolated tornado still not out of the \nquestion. The bigger concern by this time with be the flash \nflood potential given the potential for training/backbuilding \nand a deep warm cloud layer wherever the composite outflow \nstalls. This placement will depend on how the late afternoon- \nearly evening activity evolves (strength of cold pool/outflow \nuncertain). This will need to be closely monitored regardless \ngiven the hybrid Maddox/frontal flash flood look to this.\n\nThis periodically stormy pattern will likely continue into at least \nMonday and Tuesday as several small scale perturbations track east-\nsoutheast in the vicinity of the lingering frontal zone and \nassociated MUCAPE axis. Forecast specifics are very uncertain in \nthis kind of pattern (take it one day at a time) given the subtle \nnature to the forcing mechanisms and the influence of previous \nconvective episodes on where exactly the frontal boundary sets up \nfor the next round. As for Monday, expect a lull Monday morning \nbefore chances for severe storms increase during the afternoon hours \nas strong diurnal boundary layer destabilization develops under 30-\n40 kts of 0-6 km shear. Damaging winds appear to the primary threat, \nwith another round of training storms and flash potential Monday \nnight as the low level jet ramps back up. \n\nTrends in guidance continue to favor a trend toward drier/less \nactive conditions mid to late week as previous episodes of \nconvection hopefully force the effective boundary off to the \nsouth.",
